,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0]],"output":[[1,0,4],[0,0,0],[8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the input is made of.** Each input is a lattice. Full lines of one colour (1, 3, 8 or 4) cut a background of 0s into small square cells. Most crossing points of the lattice have the line colour. A few crossing points carry a different colour (6/3, 2/8, 1/2, 1/3), and they always sit on lattice intersections.\n\n**What the output is.** Each output is a 3×3 grid. Consider a lattice cell, meaning the 0-region between two adjacent line rows and two adjacent line columns. If all four corner intersections of that cell share the same special colour, the cell takes that colour. Otherwise it stays 0. The output keeps only the window of lattice cells spanned by the special intersections.\n\n**Checking this against the demonstrations:**\n- **Pair 1.** Colour 6 is at line rows 11 and 14, line columns 14, 17 and 20. That fully encloses two cells, giving 6 6 in the top row. Colour 3 is at rows 17 and 20, columns 14 to 23, giving three cells: 3 3 3 in the bottom row. The middle band (rows 14 to 17) has mixed corners (6 on top, 3 below), so it becomes 0 0 0. The special intersections span line rows 11 to 20 and line columns 14 to 23, which is 3×3 cells. This matches the output.\n- **Pair 2.** The 2-corners enclose two cells: (rows 7–11, cols 7–11) and (rows 11–15, cols 3–7). The 8-corners enclose one cell (rows 15–19, cols 11–15). The window is rows 7–19 and cols 3–15, so the output is `0 2 0 / 2 0 0 / 0 0 8`. This matches.\n- **Pair 3.** The four 1s enclose the top-left cell. The 2s enclose the right column and the bottom row. This gives `1 0 2 / 0 0 2 / 2 2 2`, which matches.\n- **Pair 4.** Colour 1 is at columns 5 and 8, rows 5 to 14, enclosing a vertical strip of three cells. Colour 3 encloses one cell at the top right. This gives `1 0 3 / 1 0 0 / 1 0 0`, which matches.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Line rows are the rows that contain no 0. Line columns are found the same way.\n2. The line colour is the most common non-zero colour sitting on intersections.\n3. Collect the intersections whose colour differs from the line colour. Their line-index span sets the output window.\n4. For each cell in the window, fill it with the corner colour if all four corners agree on a special colour.\n\n**Applying this to the test input.**\n- Colour 1 sits at rows 4 and 9, columns 4 and 9, giving one cell at the top left.\n- Colour 4 sits at rows 4 and 9, columns 14 and 19, giving one cell at the top right.\n- Colour 8 sits at rows 14 and 19, columns 4 to 19, giving a full bottom row.\n- The window is line rows 4–19 and line columns 4–19, which is 3×3 cells.\n- Expected output: `1 0 4 / 0 0 0 / 8 8 8`.\n\n\n\nNote: `Counter` comes from `collections`. If it is not pre-imported in your environment, add `from collections import Counter` at the top of `transform`.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    lrows = [y for y in range(H) if np.all(g[y] != 0)]\n    lcols = [x for x in range(W) if np.all(g[:, x] != 0)]\n    inter = [g[y, x] for y in lrows for x in lcols]\n    line_col = Counter(inter).most_common(1)[0][0]\n    special = [(i, j) for i, y in enumerate(lrows) for j, x in enumerate(lcols)\n               if g[y, x] != line_col]\n    r0 = min(i for i, _ in special); r1 = max(i for i, _ in special)\n    c0 = min(j for _, j in special); c1 = max(j for _, j in special)\n    h, w = max(r1 - r0, 1), max(c1 - c0, 1)\n    out = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=int)\n    emit(out, f\"Allocate a {h}x{w} grid: one cell per lattice cell spanned by the special-coloured intersections.\")\n    for a in range(h):\n        for b in range(w):\n            i, j = r0 + a, c0 + b\n            corners = [g[lrows[i], lcols[j]], g[lrows[i], lcols[j + 1]],\n                       g[lrows[i + 1], lcols[j]], g[lrows[i + 1], lcols[j + 1]]]\n            if len(set(corners)) == 1 and corners[0] != line_col:\n                out[a, b] = corners[0]\n                emit(out, f\"Lattice cell ({a},{b}) has all four corners coloured {corners[0]}, so it is filled with {corners[0]}.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: cells without four matching special corners stay 0.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The line colour is the most common non-zero colour sitting on intersections."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"That fully encloses two cells, giving 6 6 in the top row."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 2.** The 2-corners enclose two cells: (rows 7–11, cols 7–11) and (rows 11–15, cols 3–7)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The 8-corners enclose one cell (rows 15–19, cols 11–15)."}],"scale":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Allocate a 3x3 grid: one cell per lattice cell spanned by the special-coloured intersections."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The middle band (rows 14 to 17) has mixed corners (6 on top, 3 below), so it becomes 0 0 0."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"488e130af4a466d1e1ab30ab24924ce530bb49f94adb80e36ce95283b9567d89","task_sha256":"cd4008dc95393c5de14ad72049f4f7264dfb6b1ae4ff6977e7d9f9ba7fde18aa","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
